  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The hyperbola ${y}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15}=1$ intersects the line $y=-1$ at\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $).$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=-1,$ we substitute $y=-1$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15} & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15} & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $y=-1$  at $(0,-1).$",
